<div class="space-y-12">
    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="border-b border-white/10 pb-8">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight lg:text-5xl mb-4">
            <span class="text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-400 via-orange-400 to-amber-400 animate-gradient-x">
                Authorization
            </span>
            <span class="text-white">&amp; Policies</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
            A Pundit-style policy layer that answers one question: is the current user allowed to perform this action on this record?
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Overview -->
    <section>
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-amber-900/20 to-orange-900/20 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-white/10 shadow-xl backdrop-blur-sm">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <div class="p-3 bg-amber-500/20 rounded-lg text-amber-400">
                    <svg class="w-8 h-8" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 15v2m-6 4h12a2 2 0 002-2v-6a2 2 0 00-2-2H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v6a2 2 0 002 2zm10-10V7a4 4 0 00-8 0v4h8z" />
                    </svg>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3 mt-0 border-none">Deny by default</h2>
                    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-0">
                        Policies start out forbidding everything — you opt in to what you want to allow. A record class with no matching policy is denied, so a forgotten rule fails closed rather than open. Scaffold the whole thing, including a <code class="text-amber-300">User</code> model and login flow, with one command.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 1. Generate -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">1</span>
            Generate it
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            <code class="text-amber-300">soli generate auth</code> scaffolds the full Devise-style auth suite &mdash; login/signup, password reset (hashed one-time tokens, 2h expiry), email confirmation with resend (with the gate on, an unconfirmed account answers a sign-in like a wrong password, so the reply never confirms the password; the emailed link shows a page whose button confirms &mdash; a CSRF-checked POST &mdash; so a mail scanner fetching the URL confirms nothing; completing a password reset confirms the address too), remember-me (HttpOnly + Secure persistent cookie, revoked on password reset), per-IP throttling on all three credential endpoints, account lockout with auto-unlock (failed attempts counted with one atomic write, so parallel guesses cannot slip past it; the owner is mailed once when it trips; an account with no password, such as a SAML <code>sso_only</code> one, counts none, so nobody can lock its owner out) &mdash; and the policy layer together. Thresholds and the confirmation gate are constants/toggles at the top of the generated <code>app/models/user.sl</code>; configure SMTP (<code>SOLI_SMTP_*</code>) so the emails go out, and set <code class="text-orange-400">APP_BASE_URL</code> to your <code>https://</code> origin &mdash; unset in production, reset and confirmation emails carry <code>http://localhost:5011</code> links. The per-IP budget is read from the environment: <code class="text-orange-400">AUTH_ATTEMPTS_PER_IP</code> per <code class="text-orange-400">AUTH_IP_WINDOW_SECONDS</code> (default 8 per 300 s — under the lockout&rsquo;s ten failures, so one address cannot lock an account in one go; unset, blank or non-positive falls back to the default).
        </p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4">The remember-me cookie has a server-side end (<code>AUTH_REMEMBER_DAYS</code> after it was issued), honours the gates of the sign-in form, and restores a session nobody just signed in to: adding a passkey, an API token or an authenticator asks for a sign-in from the last 15 minutes (<code>AUTH_FRESH_SECONDS</code>). The password lockout closes the password only &mdash; passkeys, sign-in links and API tokens keep working, since anyone who knows an address can send ten wrong passwords. Small writes to the user touch their own fields, so a sign-in that straddles a password reset cannot put the old password back.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4"><strong class="text-white">Open sign-up gives one answer.</strong> With <code>AUTH_SIGNUP=open</code>, <code>/signup</code> answers "check your mailbox" whether the address was free or already had an account, and signs nobody in: a new account gets a confirmation link and signs in once it is confirmed, the owner of an existing one gets a note (<code>AuthMailer.already_registered</code>). "Could not create your account" told anyone which addresses are registered &mdash; and so did signing in straight after signing up, which is why open sign-up always confirms first. <code>first_user</code> is unchanged: the first account is signed in at once.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4"><strong class="text-white">Answers that take the same time.</strong> The mails of the uniform-answer endpoints (reset, confirmation, sign-in link, lockout notice) are sent off the request (<code>deliver_later</code>), and an address with no account costs the same database write (<code>User.burn_write_work</code>) and the same Argon2 work as one that has: neither the reply nor its timing says whether an address is registered.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4"><strong class="text-white">The remember-me token is used once.</strong> Each time it restores a session it is replaced; the one it replaced stays good for a minute, for requests a browser sent side by side. Seen again later, it means two parties hold the cookie &mdash; a copy: remember-me ends for the account, <strong class="text-white">every session is ended</strong> and the owner is mailed. The replacement is a compare-and-swap.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4"><strong class="text-white">A link someone else asks for does not cancel yours.</strong> Anyone can ask for a reset, a confirmation or a sign-in link for any address. A reset or a confirmation link sent in the last five minutes (<code>AUTH_RESEND_SECONDS</code>) stays the one that works &mdash; a new request sends nothing, with the same answer &mdash; and sign-in links stay pending side by side and asking never removes one that is waiting; a request is refused instead &mdash; silently, with the same answer &mdash; past <code>MAGIC_LINKS_PER_REQUESTER</code> (3) for one requester (an IPv4 address, an IPv6 /64) or <code>MAGIC_LINKS_PENDING</code> (100) for the account. A browser that asks again holds the nonces of its last few requests, so the link already in the mailbox still opens; a nonce is never reused for a new link. The browser cookie is <code>__Host-magic_link</code>: a sibling subdomain cannot plant it. Before, a request sent a second after the owner&rsquo;s replaced their link, for as long as someone kept asking.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4"><strong class="text-white">One way to open a session.</strong> Every sign-in &mdash; password, reset, link, passkey, second factor, provider, SAML, remember-me &mdash; goes through <code>auth_open_session(user)</code>, which gives the session a new id and drops what its previous occupant left: an impersonation, a SAML expiry, and the two-factor answer. That last one mattered: a browser that had signed in to an account before it turned two-factor on kept "this account has none", and signing in again with the password skipped the challenge.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4"><strong class="text-white">An account nobody ever entered is taken over by the first proof of its address.</strong> Someone can create an account with your address and their password, and wait for you to confirm it. So the confirmation page of an account that was never signed in to (<code>User#never_signed_in?</code>) asks for the password chosen at sign-up: given, the same person did both, and it stays. Without it, or with another one, the confirmation revokes everything the account could be entered with and <strong class="text-white">mails</strong> a link to choose a password &mdash; mailed, not handed to that browser, so a forwarded confirmation mail is not a way in. A sign-in link and a provider sign-in do the same. An account that has been in use is only confirmed: a mailbox alone never removes its password or its second factor. A confirmation link is dead once the account is confirmed another way.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4"><strong class="text-white">Answers take the same time.</strong> The replies that must not tell a registered address from an unknown one (reset, confirmation, sign-in link, open sign-up) are held until <code>AUTH_UNIFORM_SECONDS</code> (a quarter of a second) have passed, whatever was done behind them.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4"><strong class="text-white">What counts as a placeholder.</strong> The sign-up form marks the account it creates (<code>awaiting_owner</code>); the first session or API token of its own clears the mark, an administrator impersonating does not. Only a marked account is taken over. An account made any other way &mdash; by an administrator, a directory, a seed, or before the mark existed &mdash; is never one, so nothing needs setting on existing accounts. Do not make <code>auth_require_confirmed_email</code> false while strangers can sign up: an account signed in to at sign-up stops being a placeholder at once, and the take-over no longer protects the address&rsquo;s owner. <strong class="text-white">A sign-in link signs nobody in by itself:</strong> it opens a page naming the account, and the button on it does &mdash; on a shared browser, a link someone else asked for and sent along would otherwise sign the next visitor in to that someone&rsquo;s account.</p>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-amber-500/5 border border-amber-500/20 p-5 mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-amber-300 mb-2">Who may sign up: <code>AUTH_SIGNUP</code></h3>
            <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sign-up is not open to anyone by default. <code class="text-amber-300">first_user</code> (the default) lets <code>/signup</code> create one account &mdash; the first, which becomes the admin &mdash; and then closes it; <code class="text-amber-300">open</code> lets anyone sign up, for a public app; <code class="text-amber-300">closed</code> lets nobody (create accounts from a console, a seed or an admin page). Any other value counts as <code>closed</code>, so a typo fails shut. While sign-up is closed, <code>/signup</code> answers <code>403</code> with the sign-in form and the &ldquo;Create one&rdquo; link is hidden. <strong class="text-white">Outside development</strong> (anything but <code>soli serve --dev</code> or <code>APP_ENV=development</code> / <code>test</code> &mdash; a deployment that forgot <code>APP_ENV=production</code> is still asked), <code class="text-amber-300">first_user</code> asks for <code class="text-amber-300">AUTH_SETUP_TOKEN</code> (16+ characters) on the sign-up form: on a freshly deployed app the first visitor to reach <code>/signup</code> became the admin. With no token set, no first account is created, and OAuth creates none either &mdash; the admin signs up at <code>/signup</code> with the token.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg bg-emerald-500/5 border border-emerald-500/20 p-5 mb-6">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-emerald-300 mb-2">Sign-in tells an attacker nothing</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An unknown email and a wrong password return the same message <em>and</em> take the same time: the miss path spends the same Argon2id work instead of returning early, so response timing is not an enumeration oracle either. The specific &ldquo;account locked&rdquo; message appears only <em>after</em> the password has been verified &mdash; the owner learns what happened, someone guessing does not learn that the address exists. <code class="text-amber-300">AUTH_MIN_PASSWORD_LENGTH</code> (default 12) is enforced through <code class="text-amber-300">User#password_error</code>, which both sign-up and password reset call, so the reset flow cannot install a password registration would have rejected.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden bg-[#1e1e1e] border border-white/10 shadow-2xl">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-3 bg-white/5 border-b border-white/5">
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-red-500/80"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-yellow-500/80"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-green-500/80"></div>
                <span class="ml-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-mono">terminal</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-6 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli generate auth
soli db:migrate up        # create the users collection
soli serve . --dev        # then visit /signup, /login</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-6">
            Files in <code class="text-amber-300">app/policies/</code> are auto-loaded into the global scope at boot, like models. Restart the server after adding a new policy.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 2. Write a policy -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">2</span>
            Write a policy
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            A policy is a class named <code class="text-amber-300">&lt;Model&gt;Policy</code> with one predicate per action: <code class="text-amber-300">index?</code>, <code class="text-amber-300">show?</code>, <code class="text-amber-300">create?</code>, <code class="text-amber-300">new?</code>, <code class="text-amber-300">update?</code>, <code class="text-amber-300">edit?</code>, <code class="text-amber-300">destroy?</code>. Inside it, <code class="text-amber-300">@user</code> is the current user and <code class="text-amber-300">@record</code> is the record being checked.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden bg-[#1e1e1e] border border-white/10 shadow-2xl">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-3 bg-white/5 border-b border-white/5">
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-red-500/80"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-yellow-500/80"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-green-500/80"></div>
                <span class="ml-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-mono">app/policies/post_policy.sl</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-6 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">class PostPolicy < ApplicationPolicy
  def show?
    true                                    # anyone may read a post
  end

  def update?
    return false unless @signed_in?   # guard the nil user first

    @user["_key"] == @record["author_id"]
  end

  def destroy?
    @update?                         # same rule as update
  end
end</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-6">
            <code class="text-amber-300">ApplicationPolicy</code> returns <code class="text-amber-300">false</code> for every predicate, so you only override what you allow. <code class="text-amber-300">new?</code> falls back to <code class="text-amber-300">create?</code> and <code class="text-amber-300">edit?</code> to <code class="text-amber-300">update?</code> unless overridden.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. Authorize -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">3</span>
            Authorize in a controller
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            Call <code class="text-amber-300">authorize(record)</code> at the top of an action. It builds the matching policy and calls the predicate for the current action — a falsey result raises <code class="text-amber-300">403 Forbidden</code>; otherwise it returns the record.
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden bg-[#1e1e1e] border border-white/10 shadow-2xl">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-3 bg-white/5 border-b border-white/5">
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-red-500/80"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-yellow-500/80"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-green-500/80"></div>
                <span class="ml-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-mono">app/controllers/posts_controller.sl</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-6 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># PATCH /posts/:id
def update
  post = Post.find(params["id"])
  authorize(post)                  # 403 unless PostPolicy#update? is true
  post.update(@_permit_params(params))
  redirect(post_path(post))
end

# Pass an explicit action when it differs from the controller action:
authorize(post, "show")</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Helpers -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Helpers</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr>
                        <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Helper</th>
                        <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Available in</th>
                        <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Returns</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-300">authorize(record, action?)</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3">controllers / policies</td><td class="px-4 py-3">the record, or raises 403</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-300">policy_for(record)</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3">controllers / policies</td><td class="px-4 py-3">the policy instance</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-300">current_user()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3">controllers / policies / views</td><td class="px-4 py-3">the signed-in User, or nil</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-300">signed_in?()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3">controllers / policies / views</td><td class="px-4 py-3">true when signed in</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-300">current_action()</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3">anywhere</td><td class="px-4 py-3">the in-flight action name</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="px-4 py-3"><code class="text-amber-300">forbidden(message?)</code></td><td class="px-4 py-3">anywhere</td><td class="px-4 py-3">raises 403 immediately</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-6">
            <code class="text-amber-300">current_user</code> is populated per request by the generated <code class="text-amber-300">load_current_user</code> middleware, which reads the user id from the session and loads the <code class="text-amber-300">User</code>. A denied request renders through the standard error pipeline, so a custom <code class="text-amber-300">app/views/errors/403.html.slv</code> is used automatically.
        </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Next -->
    <section>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
            <a href="/docs/security/authentication" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">← Authentication with JWT</a>
            <a href="/docs/security/sessions" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">Sessions →</a>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
